 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board and receive committed changes over self-hosted WebSockets. Socket delivery provides fast updates without recurring realtime-service fees or provider coupling, in exchange for operating connection capacity and reconnect handling ourselves.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The change store provides replayable revisions and reliable publication of committed changes. Delivery manages socket sessions and publication to connected browsers. Browsers reconnect after connectivity failures and catch up from their last received revision. Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together around those revision and recovery contracts.
 
